3. Solids, Liquids, and Gases
Most substances can be described as existing in two states—solid and fluid. An element of solid has a preferred shape, to which it relaxes when the external forces on it are withdrawn. In contrast, a fluid does not have any preferred shape. Consider a rectangular element of solid ABCD (Figure 1.1a). Under the action of a shear force F the element assumes the shape ABC′D′. If the solid is perfectly elastic, it goes back to its preferred shape ABCD when F is withdrawn. In contrast, a fluid deforms


